# XBD 4.4 Directory Operations
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Atomic and serializable: the tests race processes against one another
# and check that what they find could have come from the operations running
# one after the other. A timing-dependent outcome that did not exercise the
# interleaving (no observer caught the change under way) is UNRESOLVED,
# never a pass. XSH.rename.* tests the rename atomicity sentences.

assertion  XBD.4.4-directory-operations.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.4
class      required
says       When six processes try at once to create the same name with
           open(O_CREAT|O_EXCL), mkdir(), link(), symlink() or mkfifo(),
           exactly one succeeds and each other fails with EEXIST (forty
           rounds of each); the name exists afterwards.
timeout    150
test       t/001-create-races.c

assertion  XBD.4.4-directory-operations.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.4
class      required
says       When six processes try at once to unlink() or rmdir() the same
           name, or to rename it away to six different names, exactly one
           succeeds and each other fails with ENOENT; afterwards the old
           name is gone and (for rename) exactly one new name exists.
timeout    150
test       t/002-remove-races.c

assertion  XBD.4.4-directory-operations.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.4
class      required
says       A directory operation that fails leaves the file system as it
           was: after sixteen failing calls (mkdir with a missing parent,
           mkdir of an existing name, rmdir of a non-empty directory,
           renames of a directory onto a non-empty one, into itself, of a
           file onto a directory and the reverse, of a missing file, link
           and symlink onto existing names, an O_EXCL|O_TRUNC open of an
           existing file, mkfifo onto an existing name, link of a missing
           file, mkdir below a file, mkdir of a name longer than {NAME_MAX})
           the names, inode numbers, modes, link counts and file sizes are
           those before.
test       t/003-failed-ops-no-effect.c

assertion  XBD.4.4-directory-operations.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.4
class      required
says       While a process renames newly made files over one name 3000
           times, three other processes opening that name always find it,
           always read a whole file (never a mixture), and never see an
           older one after a newer one.
timeout    150
test       t/004-rename-replace.c

assertion  XBD.4.4-directory-operations.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.4
class      required
says       The directory operations of one process are seen in their order
           by another: when names f000 to f299 are created one after
           another, a scan from f299 down to f000 that finds a name finds
           all the lower ones; when they are removed one after another, a
           scan from the top that misses a name misses all the lower ones.
           UNRESOLVED when no scan caught the change under way.
timeout    150
test       t/005-serial-order.c
